    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d804">

                <pb facs="0926" n="914" sameAs="frus:frus1942v01/pg914"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/19354</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Berle</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-01-28">January 28, 1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary</hi>: I telephoned the President today
                    in respect of the St. Lawrence Waterway. While on the telephone I told him that
                    we had news that Thailand had declared war;<anchor n="6" xml:id="d804-f1"/> but
                    that you had approved the policy of ignoring the matter. The President said he
                    cordially agreed.</p>
                <p>He then said that he thought it would be a good idea if you were to write a note
                    to the Chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee of the Senate and the
                    Chairman of the Foreign Affairs Committee of the House,<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d804-f2"/> stating quite briefly that the Department had been
                    informed that declarations of war on the United States had been made by Hungary,
                    Rumania, Bulgaria<anchor n="8" xml:id="d804-f3"/> and Thailand; and that no
                    action appeared to be required.</p>
                <p>I said I would pass the suggestion along. The President’s idea was that this
                    would merely complete the record and treat the declarations with the contempt
                    they deserved.</p>
                <closer><signed>A. A. B[<hi rendition="#small-caps">erle</hi>], <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">Jr.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d804-f1" n="6" xml:id="d804-f1-content">
                <p>January 25; for official notification, see telegram No. 350, February 2, from the
                    Chargé in Switzerland, <ref target="#pg915">p. 915</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d804-f2" n="7" xml:id="d804-f2-content">
                <p>Letters of February 7 to Senator Tom Connally and Representative Sol Bloom not
                    printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d804-f3" n="8" xml:id="d804-f3-content">
                <p>Hungarian and Bulgarian declarations made on December 13, 1941; Rumanian
                    declaration on December 12, 1941.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
